"""
# `rt_tls13_sha256` must produce the FIPS 180-4 digest on BOTH engines

Regression guard for
`doc/08_tracking/bug/jit_rt_tls13_sha256_returns_empty_2026-08-05.md`.

## What went wrong

`rt_tls13_sha256` is an interpreter-table-only extern: `nm` on the seed binary
shows `T rt_text_to_bytes` but NO global `rt_tls13_sha256` (only the local,
Rust-mangled `interpreter_extern::tls13::rt_tls13_sha256`). So the hybrid
transform rewrote every call to it into `MirInst::InterpCall`.

`compilability.rs::return_type_keeps_boxed` decided whether that `InterpCall`
keeps its result boxed. It had arms for `Type::Tuple` and text only -- NOT for
`Type::Array` -- so `boxed_result` was `false` for `-> [u8]`. With the name
starting `rt_`, `codegen/instr/core.rs::compile_interp_call` then applied
`rt_value_raw_i64` to the marshalled heap array, stripping its tag. The caller
read `len() == 0` for EVERY input, silently, exit 0, on the DEFAULT engine of
`bin/simple run`.

## Why this cannot be an ordinary in-process `expect`

A spec body never reaches the Cranelift JIT: `describe`/`it`/`expect` are Rust
interpreter intrinsics with no JIT lowering, and a spec file has no `fn main`,
so it is routed to `evaluate_module` regardless of `SIMPLE_EXECUTION_MODE`.
An in-process assertion here would have been green throughout the defect.

The block below therefore spawns a runnable `fn main` probe under an
explicitly NAMED engine and scores its self-reported VERDICT LINE, never the
exit code -- the defect produced exit 0 with an empty digest.

## Proven to fail

Run against the pre-fix seed the JIT arm reports
`PROBE VERDICT: FAIL rt_tls13_sha256 failed 3/3 FIPS 180-4 vectors`
while the interpret arm reports PASS. This guard was observed RED before it
was trusted.

@tag:crypto
@tag:regression
"""

use std.spec
use std.spec.engine_probe.{engine_stdout}

val _PROBE = "src/app/test/jit_tls13_sha256_fips_kat_probe.spl"
val _PASS = "PROBE VERDICT: PASS"

describe "rt_tls13_sha256 matches the published FIPS 180-4 vectors on both engines":
    """
    As a maintainer of the std TLS 1.3 and sshd kex digest channel, I need the
    one-shot SHA-256 runtime extern checked against published known-answer
    vectors under each NAMED engine, so that an engine-specific wrong answer
    cannot ship behind a spec that only ever ran on the interpreter.

    Vectors are FIPS 180-4 / NIST CAVP, not hand-computed. Helper tier:
    src/lib/nogc_sync_mut/spec/engine_probe.spl
    """
    it "matches all three vectors under the interpreter":
        # Control column: the interpreter was correct throughout the defect,
        # so this arm going red means the probe or harness broke, not codegen.
        expect(engine_stdout(_PROBE, "interpret")).to_contain(_PASS)

    it "matches all three vectors under the cranelift JIT":
        # The arm that was RED. Pre-fix this stdout carried
        # "FAIL rt_tls13_sha256 failed 3/3 FIPS 180-4 vectors".
        expect(engine_stdout(_PROBE, "jit")).to_contain(_PASS)

    it "does not report an empty digest under the cranelift JIT":
        # Names the exact symptom independently of the KAT comparison: a
        # 0-length digest is the signature of the InterpCall unbox stripping
        # the array tag. `len=0` must never appear.
        expect(engine_stdout(_PROBE, "jit")).to_not_contain("len=0 ")
